---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
const translations = [
  [
    '“It feels too busy.”',
    'Reduce competing emphasis before removing useful information. Replace a container with a rule. Keep one dominant element per section.',
  ],
  [
    '“Make it feel more human.”',
    'Name the person’s decision. Use a concrete sentence and a real example. Do not add a mascot, soft gradient, or a third font.',
  ],
  [
    '“It needs more punch.”',
    'Strengthen the claim and its typographic contrast. Give it more room. Do not make everything amber or heavier.',
  ],
  [
    '“Show me what it actually does.”',
    'Use a real capture, explain what to inspect, and let the reader open it at full size. Label any illustrative example.',
  ],
  [
    '“This should feel like the rest.”',
    'Reuse the same margin, index, type roles, action hierarchy, and evidence treatment. Reuse the grammar, not the entire layout.',
  ],
  [
    '“Make it simpler.”',
    'Shorten the route to the next decision. Move technical depth to the workbench or docs; keep important limitations visible.',
  ],
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The working record — Phosphor’s design language"
  description="A design language for human-agent iteration: clear hierarchy, real evidence, deliberate color, and a shared vocabulary for refining Phosphor in plain language."
>
  <header class="page-intro container">
    <p class="label muted">Phosphor / Design language / Edition 01</p>
    <h1 class="display">The working record.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A language for software made in conversation. Clear enough to direct. Concrete enough to
      inspect. Consistent enough to revise without starting over.
    </p>
  </header>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">01 /</span><p class="label">Who it is for</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content prose">
        <h2 class="title">People who delegate<br />work, not judgment.</h2>
        <p>
          Developers, technical founders, and small software teams working with coding agents. They
          know what a branch and a diff are. They want to move between direction, implementation,
          and review without losing the thread.
        </p>
        <p>
          They do not need another promise of autonomous magic. They need to see what happened, know
          what needs attention, and have a good place to make the next call.
        </p>
        <div class="language-example">
          <p class="label">The central promise</p><p>Keep the work in your hands.</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          This is not a claim that every tool call waits for approval. The agent executes with full
          permissions. The promise is visibility and the ability to steer, inspect, revise, and
          revert.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section work-surface">
    <div class="container section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">02 /</span><p class="label">The grammar</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2 class="title">A few rules.<br />A recognizable whole.</h2>
        <dl class="fact-list">
          <div>
            <dt>Paper for reading. Graphite for work.</dt><dd>
              The website opens on the brand’s cool slate-paper theme. Warm graphite marks a working
              passage or holds the real app. Surface changes signal a change in purpose, not a
              stripe pattern.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Amber means something.</dt><dd>
              Ember on paper; phosphor amber on graphite. Reserve it for the next action, a selected
              view, or a directional cue. No glow behind screenshots, no gradient scenery.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Two voices. Two jobs.</dt><dd>
              Inter carries the argument. JetBrains Mono carries the index, label, and command.
              Large headlines are sparse and sentence case. No ornamental serif, terminal cosplay,
              or font per section.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Margins, not containers.</dt><dd>
              An index in the left margin. The argument and evidence in the main column. Hairline
              rules separate ideas; spacing groups them. A box must contain a genuine object or
              control, not just a paragraph.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Proof has a caption.</dt><dd>
              Real screenshots stay intact, with their own chrome and a description of what matters.
              No fictional metrics, decorative dashboards, or made-up customer logos.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Motion answers an action.</dt><dd>
              The reader changes a view; the view changes. No entrance choreography, autoplay tour,
              orbiting decoration, or hidden-until-scroll copy. Every page remains readable without
              JavaScript.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">03 /</span><p class="label">Talk in plain language</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content">
        <h2 class="title">You describe the feeling.<br />We change the rule.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Feedback should not require design vocabulary. Translate a reaction into a specific
          adjustment, try it in context, and keep the change only if the whole page gets clearer.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead
              ><tr><th scope="col">What you might say</th><th scope="col">What we change</th></tr
              ></thead
            ><tbody
              >{
                translations.map(([said, change]) => (
                  <tr>
                    <>
                      <th scope="row">{said}</th>
                      <td>{change}</td>
                    </>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }</tbody
            >
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="language-example">
          <p class="label">The revision loop</p><p>
            Describe → translate → compare → keep or revise.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">
          Change one design variable at a time. Compare desktop and mobile. When a change works,
          update the shared rule and every affected page—not just the screenshot under discussion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="number">04 /</span><p class="label">Every page after</p>
      </div>
      <div class="section-content prose">
        <h2 class="title">Same language.<br />Different conversations.</h2>
        <p>
          A homepage earns interest. A workbench page explains a workflow. A download page removes
          uncertainty. A guide helps someone finish a task. Their purpose changes; their visual
          grammar does not.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Name the reader’s question.</strong> Write one sentence that the page must answer
            before designing a section.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Choose the evidence.</strong> A capture for a workflow, a table for a comparison,
            a command for setup. Never a card grid by default.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Use the shared composition.</strong> Header, generous opening, numbered margin, readable
            main column, clear next action, quiet footer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep the hierarchy honest.</strong> One filled action per viewport, ordinary navigation
            as links, labels subordinate to the argument.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check the edges.</strong> Keyboard, narrow screens, long labels, reduced motion, no
            JavaScript, failed clipboard access, and accurate product claims.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The existing Phosphor mark and brand colors remain the foundation. This is the website’s
          expression of the identity, not a redesign of the desktop app.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="closing container">
    <div>
      <p class="label">The language, in practice</p><h2 class="title">Start with the work.</h2>
    </div><a class="text-link" href="/">See the homepage <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
